# Commit 11: Give AMQP placeholders non-empty, format-valid defaults

**Branch**: `Work-on-Parameters-and-Remaining-Components`
**Scope**: `packages/compiler` (the `@cpi-ai/compiler` package)
**Audience**: same as commits 1-10 -- written for both future Claude sessions
and human contributors, explaining the evidence and reasoning behind each
decision.

---

## 1. What broke (again) -- and why this is a genuinely new finding

Commit 10 fixed a generated AMQP flow to register `{{...}}` placeholders as
real `parameters.propdef`/`param_references` entries, on the theory that
this registration alone would make SAP Integration Suite exempt those
fields from mandatory/format validation. That theory was itself **directly
falsified** by a second live SAP import: the user uploaded a fresh
generated ZIP (`flow (29).zip`) that had the *exact correct* registration
structure from commit 10 -- `<parameter>` definitions, `<reference>`
entries, everything -- and SAP **still** reported all four fields as
invalid (now including `destinationName`/"Queue Name" too, which was also
placeholder'd this time):

> Enter a value between 1 and 65535.
> Attribute 'Queue Name' is mandatory
> Enter a valid address, for example http(s)://example  *(HTTP receiver -- separate, pre-existing, out of scope; see §6)*
> Attribute 'Host' is mandatory
> Attribute 'Credential Name' is mandatory

## 2. The real root cause

Externalizing a field (registering it in `parameters.propdef`) does **not**
exempt it from SAP's mandatory/format validation. SAP resolves
`{{PARAM_NAME}}` against `parameters.prop` and validates the **resolved**
value -- externalization only makes that value *swappable later* via
Configure > Externalized Parameters, it never means "this can be blank for
now." Commit 10's fix wrote an **empty** default (`AMQP_HOST=`) for every
placeholder, so SAP resolved `{{AMQP_HOST}}` to `""` and correctly flagged
it as missing -- the same error as before, just routed through a
structurally-correct-but-still-empty parameter instead of a bare literal.

This is confirmed directly by re-examining `amqp_reference.zip`'s own
`parameters.prop`: every one of its 38 parameters, including
`EMHOST`/`EMPORT`/`EMUser`, has a **real, non-empty value** already filled
in. The reference flow was never "blank and still valid" -- it was already
fully configured before being exported. There is no SAP mechanism for
shipping a mandatory field with genuinely no value, externalized or not.

---

## 3. The fix

`ExternalizedParameter` gained a `defaultValue` field. `IflowPackager`
now writes `KEY=<defaultValue>` instead of `KEY=` to `parameters.prop`.
Per the task's explicit constraint ("do not hardcode a real company
hostname or credential"), every default is one of:

| Property | Default | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `port` | `443` | The one universal, non-tenant-specific value already confirmed by evidence (`amqp_reference.zip`'s own `EMPORT=443` -- the standard Event Mesh AMQP1.0-over-WSS port for every tenant, not this one customer's private data). |
| `path` | `/protocols/amqp10ws` | Also evidenced as the fixed, protocol-defined WebSocket path -- not tenant-specific. |
| `authentication` | `Transport_OAuth2` | An evidenced *valid selection* for this combobox-style field (a choice of auth mechanism, not a secret). |
| `deliveryState` | `REJECTED` | Same reasoning -- an evidenced valid selection, not tenant data. |
| `host` | `your-event-mesh-host.example.com` | Genuinely tenant-specific -- uses this codebase's own existing convention (the reserved `example.com` domain, already used throughout this compiler's capability examples and tests) for "obviously not real." |
| `credentialName` | `REPLACE_WITH_CREDENTIAL_NAME` | Genuinely tenant-specific -- an unmistakable placeholder string, not a real credential name. |
| `destinationName` | `REPLACE_WITH_QUEUE_NAME` | Genuinely tenant-specific -- same reasoning. |
| `connectWithTLS`/`disableReplyTo`/`consumeExpiredMessages`/`numberConcurrentProcesses`/`maxRetries`/`queuePrefetch` | Same values `AmqpAdapter` already defaults to for the non-placeholder case (`true`/`false`/`false`/`1`/`1`/`5`) | Consistency -- these were never tenant secrets in the first place. |

**This design decision was confirmed with the user before implementing**,
given two consecutive incorrect assumptions about SAP's validation
behavior on this exact feature -- see the conversation's `AskUserQuestion`
exchange. The chosen option ("obvious dummy placeholder text") was
selected over "drop `{{...}}` support entirely" and "user specifies exact
values."

`AmqpAdapter.ts` itself is **completely unchanged** -- it already correctly
writes `{{...}}` into the `.iflw` and requires host/port/credentialName to
be present (empty-string rejection, port range checking). This fix lives
entirely in `ExternalizedParameters.ts`'s default-value table and
`IflowPackager`'s one-line change to use it.

---

## 4. Files changed (this package only)

**Modified**:
- `src/utils/ExternalizedParameters.ts` -- `ExternalizedParameter.defaultValue`
  field; `AMQP_PLACEHOLDER_DEFAULTS` table; module doc rewritten with an
  explicit "IMPORTANT CORRECTION" section documenting why the previous
  approach was wrong (kept in place rather than silently deleted, so a
  future reader understands why an empty default was tried and rejected).
- `src/packager/IflowPackager.ts` -- one-line change:
  `${p.paramKey}=` → `${p.paramKey}=${p.defaultValue}`; doc comment updated.
- `src/api/capabilities.ts` -- AMQP Sender's `notes` field updated to
  describe the default-value behavior accurately.
- `test-amqp-regression.ts` -- scenario [12]'s assertion that the default
  was empty (now provably wrong) replaced with assertions on the exact new
  default values, plus a check that the real reference tenant's hostname
  never appears in generated output.

**Deliberately not touched**: `AmqpAdapter.ts`, `validate.ts`'s AD-012
through AD-015 checks, every other adapter/component type, the
sequence-flow fix, the Fiori UI, and the CAP controller/`downloadFlow`
handling.

---

## 5. Testing

Updated `test-amqp-regression.ts` scenario [12] now asserts the exact
resolved values (`AMQP_DESTINATION=REPLACE_WITH_QUEUE_NAME`,
`AMQP_HOST=your-event-mesh-host.example.com`, `AMQP_PORT=443`,
`AMQP_CREDENTIAL=REPLACE_WITH_CREDENTIAL_NAME`) and explicitly confirms the
real reference tenant's hostname
(`enterprise-messaging-messaging-gateway...`) never leaks into generated
output. Scenario [13] (literal, non-placeholder AMQP values → unchanged
byte-for-byte baseline) is unaffected and still passes, confirming this
fix only changes behavior for the placeholder path.

Additionally, directly regenerated the exact reported prompt's scenario
("Create an iFlow that receives messages from an AMQP queue and sends them
to an HTTP endpoint," using `{{AMQP_DESTINATION}}`/`{{AMQP_HOST}}`/
`{{AMQP_PORT}}`/`{{AMQP_CREDENTIAL}}` exactly as `flow (29).zip` did) and
confirmed the new `parameters.prop` output directly.

**Full pre-existing regression suite re-run after this change -- zero
regressions**: all 23 AMQP scenarios, `test-jms-regression.ts` (12),
`test-sequence-flow-regression.ts`, `test-rfc-regression.ts` (15),
`test-jdbc-regression.ts`, `test-jdbc-ai-scenarios-regression.ts`,
`test-naming-version-regression.ts`, `test-customer-flow-regression.ts`,
`test-processdirect-regression.ts`,
`test-localintegrationprocess-regression.ts`, `test/run-rt-003-tests.ts`
(9/9), `test/run-mapping-regression-simple.ts` (3/3).

---

## 6. Known limitations (unchanged from commit 10, plus one new item)

- **Still not re-verified against a live SAP Integration Suite import in
  this session.** Given this exact feature has now had two consecutive
  assumptions falsified by the user's own live tenant, this is the
  strongest possible caveat: **please re-import a freshly generated AMQP
  flow and confirm the three AMQP errors are actually gone before treating
  this as resolved.** If they persist even with a non-empty, correctly
  registered value, that would mean a third, different mechanism is at
  play (e.g. the `attribute_category`/`attribute_id` values not matching
  what SAP expects in a way the reference happens to share coincidentally,
  or something about the property's position/order in the `.iflw`) and
  would need fresh evidence to diagnose, not another guess.
- **The HTTP receiver's "Enter a valid address" error from the same
  screenshot is a separate, pre-existing, out-of-scope issue** -- it means
  the AI-generated JSON's HTTP receiver `config.url` was left empty or
  unset. This compiler's `validate()` already has an evidenced check for
  this (`AD-008`), but it is a **warning**, not an error, matching
  documented SAP behavior that an unconfigured HTTP receiver address is an
  acceptable "configure later" template-flow state (see commit 1's
  `commit1_jdbc-support-and-naming-fixes.readme`, §2d). If SAP's live
  behavior for THIS case also turns out to be stricter than that existing
  evidence suggests, that would be a separate investigation, explicitly
  not addressed here per "do not change unrelated adapters."
- The other AMQP placeholder defaults (`path`, `authentication`,
  `deliveryState`, and the six tuning-knob properties) were not part of the
  reported error and are unverified against a live import in this session
  either -- only `destinationName`/`host`/`port`/`credentialName` were
  directly confirmed broken and are now directly re-tested.
